1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
 Hello and welcome back. In this lecture, we will play with our simple MCP server and all the theory we covered in the previous lecture will make sense now.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:14,000
 Let's go through the code step-by-step and line-by-line.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:21,000
 Anthropic, which is the leading company behind MCP, has a Python SDK called MCP.

4
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:26,000
 This library will allow you to start an MCP server in a matter of minutes.

5
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:35,000
 If you have installed the dependencies from requirements.txt while, then you are good to go and you should have the MCP package already installed.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
 We start by defining a class called Contentful MCP server.

7
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:45,000
 Then we instantiate the fast MCP class which is offered by the MCP package.

8
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:54,000
 We name our MCP server as Contentful MCP server and we declare some variables for Contentful Access Token and Space ID.

9
00:00:54,000 --> 00:01:00,000
 We also define the base URL which is used for Contentful API services.

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:06,000
 We expect the user to pass the Contentful Access Token and Space ID as environment variable.

11
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:12,000
 If we scroll down a little bit under the Run method, we try to read those from environment variable.

12
00:01:12,000 --> 00:01:19,000
 And we check if the token and space ID are set, if not, then we return an error message.

13
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:29,000
 Then finally, we call the Run method which is to start the MCP server and we set the Transport mode to be standard input output.

14
00:01:29,000 --> 00:01:38,000
 Remember, we said before when we connect Cloud Disk Top to a local MCP server, then the communication channel is standard input output.

15
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:44,000
 And since this is a local MCP server, we define the Transport to be standard input output.

16
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:50,000
 Now at this point we have the bare minimum code required to start a local MCP server.

17
00:01:50,000 --> 00:01:55,000
 Now we need to set up tools that the MCP server will expose to the MCP client.

18
00:01:55,000 --> 00:02:04,000
 MCP server tools are basically just a Python function that the MCP client can call to perform specific tasks.

19
00:02:04,000 --> 00:02:11,000
 For example, our local MCP server will offer two tools or two methods if you want to call them.

20
00:02:11,000 --> 00:02:18,000
 It will offer less Contentful entries and this tool, it will list all the entries in the Contentful space.

21
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:27,000
 And also it will offer Get Contentful entry and this tool will get a specific entry by providing an entry ID.

22
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:34,000
 The MCP client can access and it can also execute these methods or these tools.

23
00:02:34,000 --> 00:02:38,000
 If the task requested by the user requires them.

24
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:44,000
 The protocol that makes it happen behind the scenes is called JSON RPC.

25
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:57,000
 JSON RPC is a remote procedure called protocol that MCP has adopted to allow the MCP client to call functions on the MCP server.

26
00:02:57,000 --> 00:03:09,000
 Using RPC protocol for the communication between the MCP client and the MCP server is a quite powerful and less complex than building for example REST API.

27
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:17,000
 With RPC you just need a single HTTP endpoint for all the functions that you want to expose and execute.

28
00:03:17,000 --> 00:03:20,000
 At the moment I am logged inside my Contentful account.

29
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:26,000
 Our MCP server should be able to list all the entries that you can see under this space.

30
00:03:26,000 --> 00:03:31,000
 But also it should be able to get a specific entry by its ID.

31
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:39,000
 So if we pass the entry ID which you can see in the URL our MCP should be able to fetch all the data under that entry.

32
00:03:39,000 --> 00:03:43,000
 Now if we go back to the code base I want to call out a few things.

33
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,000
 First notice that we use MCP dot tool as a decorator to define that this function is actually an MCP tool.

34
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:03,000
 This decorator come from the fast MCP library and it does all the heavy lifting behind the scenes to expose that this function is a tool that MCP client can call.

35
00:04:03,000 --> 00:04:21,000
 My second note we need to define the documentation string under the function and this is very important because the description that we define here it will be used by the AI model to decide which tool from the MCP server to call or execute to fit the user request.

36
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:27,000
 And my last call out notice that this function actually can take a user input as an argument.

37
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:32,000
 In this case in this case the user input is actually the entry ID.

38
00:04:32,000 --> 00:04:39,000
 And as you know in security whenever you see a user input you should immediately start thinking of an attack victor.

39
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:47,000
 I don't want to get ahead of myself but whether you are a developer security engineer penetration tester bug bounty whatever.

40
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:51,000
 I want to think of some attack victors that might happen here.

41
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:58,000
 We will touch on this later in the course but it's always good to start thinking what can go wrong from the beginning.

42
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:09,000
 We said before that the MCP server takes care of all the details like passing the authorization token header, great limit error handling and data formatting.

43
00:05:09,000 --> 00:05:17,000
 So as you can see under the implementation of each tool we simply make an HTTP request to the Contentful API.

44
00:05:17,000 --> 00:05:25,000
 We pass the token and we return the JSON response to the MCP client in case the entry has been retrieved successfully.

45
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:28,000
 Otherwise we just return the error with an error message.

46
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:42,000
 Okay before we finish off this lecture let's quickly recap and try to visualize the data flow from beginning.

47
00:05:42,000 --> 00:05:48,000
 When a user asks the model to list all the Contentful entries the AI model will check if there is an MCP server integration that can handle this request then it finds the Contentful MCP server.

48
00:05:48,000 --> 00:05:57,000
 Then using the tool documentation string as we said it will decide to call the list Contentful entries tool.

49
00:05:57,000 --> 00:06:03,000
 Then using the MCP client it will send an HTTP request to the MCP server.

50
00:06:03,000 --> 00:06:12,000
 The payload of this HTTP request it will contain the name of the tool that we want to call as well as any provided user input.

51
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:31,000
 Now when the MCP server receives this request it will execute the list Contentful entries function which makes an API request to Contentful API and then it will retain the response back to the MCP client which will pass the response back to the AI model.

52
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:44,000
 In the next lecture we will integrate this local MCP server with the cloud disk top and you will see this flow in action. Alright guys I hope that this lecture has been informative for you and I would love to see you in the next one.

53
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:45,000
 Thanks for watching.

